{"rule":"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged. The text field holds, in at most 1800 characters, everything a reader would need to know of the story so far, plus one new event that moves the story forward. It is written for a human reader.","text":"At Vardnes, Marit Solvang, forty-seven, watched the tide swallow her mussel bucket at 13:09—fifty-one minutes before the predicted high water. The sea had already reached the shed doorstep, forcing her to move the outboard motor to the loft. The cottage barometer remained fixed at 1003, as it had all week. On the breakwater three gulls perched above a fourth lying dead on the shingle.

Anders Holm, the lighthouse keeper, had left on March 2. His cottage key hung behind Marit's door, and his logbook documented tides arriving progressively earlier since the previous July: first four minutes, then nine, then twenty-two. Marit's grandmother Signe had kept the tide table for forty years; this was no familiar error. In a village of 212, help was limited. The ferry Kittiwake passed the point at 16:40 on Tuesdays and Fridays, while her brother Tomas, working at a fish plant in Bergen, usually called on Sundays.

Before dark, Marit took the logbook and Anders's key to the cottage. Inside, she found a sheet pinned beneath the kitchen clock: a tide table in Anders's handwriting, extending three weeks beyond his departure. Beside today's date he had written 13:09. The next entry was not a time but an instruction: WHEN THE WELL TURNS SALT, LEAVE BEFORE THE BELL."}
